1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,600
This looks great on the graph,

2
00:00:02,850 --> 00:00:08,050
but how do we know it is a real
phenomenon that goes on to infinity,

3
00:00:08,300 --> 00:00:12,300
and not something that only looks
right in the range we checked?

4
00:00:12,750 --> 00:00:14,950
How do we prove it?

5
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:19,800
And come to think of it,
what on earth do we need to prove here?

6
00:00:20,250 --> 00:00:23,450
Well, the last question
is fairly easy to answer.

7
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:27,900
We want to compare our
function with ln(n).

8
00:00:28,350 --> 00:00:32,550
One way to do this is to
divide one by the other,

9
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:37,400
and we want to say that this
ratio is very close to 1.

10
00:00:37,850 --> 00:00:42,850
Of course, when n is small,
our statistics are not very good yet,

11
00:00:43,100 --> 00:00:44,900
and there can be a deviation.

12
00:00:45,350 --> 00:00:48,750
But as n grows,
the deviation tends to vanish.

13
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:51,800
That's what limits were invented for.

14
00:00:52,250 --> 00:00:55,650
So our claim is really
that the limit is 1.

15
00:00:56,100 --> 00:00:59,300
And as we said,
any other percentage works too.

